## Introduction

Exterior screeding is a different discipline from interior work. The wall faces equatorial sun, driving rain, and daily thermal movement — a finish that survives comfortably inside a bedroom will chalk, craze and let water in within two seasons outside unless the approach changes.

This guide covers exterior screeding specifically: surface preparation for weather exposure, mix adjustments for external conditions, thickness and reinforcement choices, sun-and-curing management, and the finishing and coating decisions that decide whether the wall weathers gracefully or becomes an annual repair line item.

## What the Outside Does to a Finish — the Three Enemies

Every exterior decision answers one of three loads:

**Sun, daily and relentless.** The surface cycles hot-to-cool every day, hundreds of times a season. Cement-rich surfaces chalk under UV; unmanaged curing in direct sun craze-maps within the first week; and dark finishes collect heat that drives the cycle harder. Exterior work is scheduled around the sun's clock, not in spite of it.

**Rain, driven and standing.** Wind-driven rain finds every lap, crack, and unbonded edge; standing water at wall bases, splashed from ground contact or collected at coping failures, does the deepest damage. Water is the enemy that tests every other defect — it finds what the eye forgave.

**Movement, thermal and structural.** Walls move daily with temperature, and cracks open and close with every cycle. An exterior coat either accommodates that movement (thickness discipline, control joints, flexible finishes) or it becomes the crack map — because the wall's movement does not negotiate with the surface's rigidity.

## Preparation Outdoors — the Same Verdicts, Stricter Standards

The [four verdicts](/learn/how-to-prepare-walls-for-screeding) apply outdoors with the stakes raised:

- **Tap:** exterior render that has taken years of sun and rain fails the tap more often than indoor work ever does — algae-holding damp patches delaminate quietly long before they show. Cut back generously; the wall you leave is the wall you inherit.
- **Wash:** exteriors carry dust, algae, and microflora — the shaded north face of a wall in humid Nigeria is a garden. Wash it, treat it, and let it dry properly; screeding over algae is seeding the failure.
- **Straightedge:** exterior planes matter for drainage as much as looks — falls away from the building, no ledges and water-traps where coating after coating fails.
- **Moisture:** after rain, the wall waits. An exterior wall that has just taken a storm holds water in its depth for days; a coat applied over it traps the storm, and the first sun blisters it back out.

One extra verdict outdoors: **the ground zone.** Wall bases that take splash-back, rising damp, or standing water need their own belt of specification (the DPC line's integrity checked — see [the construction mistakes guide](/learn/common-construction-mistakes-and-how-to-prevent) for the DPC failure that no exterior finish can outrun).

## Mix and Thickness for the Outdoors

The exterior mix logic inverts the interior instinct:

- **Leaner, not richer.** Where indoor finishing trends richer at the surface for hardness, exterior bulk coats trend the other way — the shrinkage of rich mixes cracks under thermal cycling, and a 1:5–1:6 render, well-cured, outlasts a 1:3 exterior pass every season it ages. Richness belongs in thin finishing passes only, if at all outdoors.
- **Well-graded, clean sand.** Exterior mixes live on their sand: the fines that make an indoor mix "smooth" add shrinkage outdoors. Washed plaster sand, graded, consistent per batch.
- **Thickness with movement in mind:** the standard render coat at 12–15 mm, in passes — not one thick hero coat whose shrinkage the sun will map. Where fills exceed 15 mm, build in passes over a keyed, cured base.
- **Reinforcement where spans and cracks demand:** mesh (chicken-gauge galvanized or the proprietary render meshes) bedded in the coat across movement zones — over dissimilar materials junctions, around openings' corners (the diagonal crack signatures), and on large uninterrupted planes. The mesh does not stop movement; it stops movement *concentrating* a crack in one place — it distributes the strain so the surface survives it.

## The Sun-and-Cure Management — Exterior's Decisive Discipline

More exterior screed dies in its first week than in its following years — killed by sun during and after application:

- **Work the clock:** east faces in the morning before their sun arrives, west faces from midday; never coat a wall about to take direct sun. The fresh coat needs hours without the surface being scorched.
- **Damp-cure longer:** mist morning and evening for 5–7 days minimum, hessian or polythene hung on the working face through the curing window. Exterior cement gain is slower when the surface cycles hot — the calendar, not the touch, declares the cure.
- **Respect the forecast:** an exterior coat needs its dry window (application) *and* its protected window (cure). A coat washed by rain within its first hours is not a coat; scheduling around the season ([the construction timeline guide](/learn/construction-timeline-how-long-each-phase-takes)) is part of the craft.
- **Harmattan is the hidden hazard:** the dry wind strips moisture faster than sun does, and the season's "perfect working weather" cures surfaces into dusting and crazing — mist more, shade more, and suspect any coating week that feels *comfortable*.

## Finishing and Coating — What Weather-Proofs the Work

The exterior finish decision is a weathering decision, not a texture preference:

- **The float-finished render, coated:** the standard — a well-cured render taking a quality exterior coating (see [interior vs exterior finishing](/learn/interior-vs-exterior-wall-finishing-differences) for the coating families). The coating is the sacrificial, renewable weather skin; the render is the permanent wall.
- **Textured and aggregate finishes:** tyrolene, broom-drag, and the textured coating systems — the honest exterior choice, because texture breaks up the surface's shadow-free uniformity, hides hairline movement, and holds up under UV far better than a smooth film (see [the textured finishes guide](/learn/achieving-textured-finishes-techniques-tools)).
- **Smooth exterior screed + paint** is the option this guide warns about: it is the bedroom finish asked to do a boundary wall's job, and its chalking, crazing, and touch-up cycles are the "annual repair line item" the intro promised. If smoothness is the brief, the answer is premium exterior systems and a maintenance calendar — the look is bought, not wished.
- **The drainage details that decide everything:** coping and capping sound, falls away at sills and ledges, the ground-zone belt specified, and every penetration (pipe, box, junction) flashed and sealed. Water that cannot stand cannot stay.

## The Exterior Inspection — Checks That Match the Exposure

Acceptance outdoors runs the indoor protocol plus its weather extras: the straightedge (with falls checked, not just flatness), the torch, the tap — plus **the hose**: water driven at the finished face for the minutes a storm will spend on it, with a watcher inside for the wall's verdict. And a calendar note instead of a handshake: the first rainy season is the exterior finish's true acceptance test, which is why a small retention through it is the professional payment structure (see [the quality control checklist](/learn/construction-quality-control-inspection-checklist)).


## Case Study — the Boundary Wall That Repainted Itself Every Year

A compound boundary wall in Lagos, rendered smooth and painted gloss-white on completion. By the first rainy season's end: hairline map cracking along the coping line and the column junctions, chalking white showing grey, and a bubble-peel band at the wall base. The owner's response, three years running: wash, patch, repaint — the "annual repair line item" at full gloss rates.

The forensic read: the **coping** had no overhang or cap — rain ran the top face and down both sides, so the wall's head was permanently damp (moisture verdict: failed every season). The **junction cracking** was thermal movement concentrated exactly where render met the columns' stiffer lines — mesh was never bedded across them. The **base band** was splash-back from unsleeved ground level hitting a wall with no ground-zone specification — the DPC-less base wicked what the splashes fed it. And the **smooth gloss finish** was the bedroom system outdoors: a tight, UV-cooked film over a moving wall — chalking and peeling was its retirement plan, not its defect.

The fix, and the cost comparison the guide exists for: crack-treat the junctions with mesh-bridged flexible bands, cap the coping with a proper overhanging capping (the one-time price that ends the moisture story), specify the base zone (a band of textured coating — see [the textured finishes guide](/learn/achieving-textured-finishes-techniques-tools)), and finish the body in a textured exterior system rather than gloss-on-smooth. The re-do cost approximately the price of the last two repaint cycles — and the wall has since passed its rainy seasons without a single touch-up. Exterior work is a specification problem before it is a product problem, and the specification is cheaper than the cycle it replaces.

## The Seasonal Calendar — When Exterior Work Happens

Nigerian exterior screeding runs on a two-season calendar, and the honest schedule plays them differently:

- **Dry season (Nov–Feb, harmattan edge):** the application window — rain-free days, but harmattan's dry wind is a curing hazard. Work the clock, mist aggressively, and shade; the season is for *coating*, not for *forgetting to cure*.
- **Early rains (Mar–Apr) and late rains (Sep–Oct):** the shoulder weeks — forecast-dependent pours, smaller bays, and the discipline of refusing a marginal day. An exterior coat needs its dry application window *and* its protected first week; either alone is a coin-flip on someone's wall.
- **Peak rains (Jun–Aug):** the honest pause for exterior faces in most of the south — assessment, prep, stripping, and interior work instead; exterior coating this season is a schedule that has chosen its weather risk on purpose.

The exterior estimate carries this calendar as a real cost line — the season's idle days and forecast waits are labour's calendar dimension, and the quote that ignores the season has not read the sky (see [the construction timeline guide](/learn/construction-timeline-how-long-each-phase-takes) for the season's effect on the whole programme).

## Closing

Exterior screeding is weathering engineering: washed and cured substrates, lean well-graded mixes built in passes, mesh across the movement zones, shade-clock application under a real damp-cure, and a finish chosen for rain and UV rather than showroom smoothness — hosed before payment and audited through the first season. Do that, and the wall weathers gracefully; skip it, and the repaint line item recurs until someone reads this guide.
